window.docContents[46].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Continuation-of-proof-of-the-Frobenius-character-formula-_LPAR_from-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Frobenius_character_formula":{"contents":"agree that U_{\\lambda+\\rho-\\sigma(\\rho)} := U_{\\langle\\lambda+\\rho-\\sigma(\\rho)\\rangle}). Now note that \\mu = \\langle\\lambda + \\rho - \\sigma(\\rho)\\rangle is obtained from \\lambda by adding vectors of the form e_i - e_j, i < j, which implies that \\mu > \\lambda or \\mu = \\lambda, and the case \\mu = \\lambda arises only if \\sigma = 1. {}^2 Therefore, the above claim follows from Proposition 5.14.1.\n\nTherefore, to show that \\theta_\\lambda = \\chi_\\lambda, by Lemma 5.7.2, it suffices to show that (\\theta_\\lambda, \\theta_\\lambda) = 1.\n\nWe have\n\n(\\theta_\\lambda, \\theta_\\lambda) = \\frac{1}{n!} \\sum_{\\mathbf{i}} |C_{\\mathbf{i}}| \\theta_\\lambda(C_{\\mathbf{i}})^2.\n\nUsing that\n\n|C_{\\mathbf{i}}| = \\frac{n!}{\\prod_m m^{i_m} i_m!},\n\nwe conclude that (\\theta_\\lambda, \\theta_\\lambda) is the coefficient of x^{\\lambda+\\rho} y^{\\lambda+\\rho} in the series R(x, y) = \\Delta(x)\\Delta(y)S(x, y), where\n\nS(x, y) = \\sum_{\\mathbf{i}} \\prod_m \\frac{(\\sum_j x_j^m)^{i_m} (\\sum_k y_k^m)^{i_m}}{m^{i_m} i_m!} = \\sum_{\\mathbf{i}} \\prod_m \\frac{(\\sum_{j,k} x_j^m y_k^m / m)^{i_m}}{i_m!}.\n\nSumming over \\mathbf{i} and m, we get\n\nS(x, y) = \\prod_m \\exp\\Bigl(\\sum_{j,k} x_j^m y_k^m / m\\Bigr)\n\n= \\exp\\Bigl(-\\sum_{j,k} \\log(1 - x_j y_k)\\Bigr) = \\prod_{j,k} (1 - x_j y_k)^{-1}.\n\nThus,\n\nR(x, y) = \\frac{\\prod_{i<j}(x_i - x_j)(y_i - y_j)}{\\prod_{i,j}(1 - x_i y_j)}.\n\nNow we need the following lemma.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.15. The Frobenius character formula\tContinuation of proof of the Frobenius character formula (from missing page)","header":"Continuation of proof of the Frobenius character formula (from missing page)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Continuation-of-proof-of-the-Frobenius-character-formula-_LPAR_from-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Frobenius_character_formula"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Examples-of-algebras-over-k/#Chapter2___Example2___2___4":{"contents":"Example 2.2.4. Here are some examples of algebras over k:\n\n1. A = k.2. A = k[x_1, \\ldots, x_n] — the algebra of polynomials in variables x_1, \\ldots, x_n.3. A = \\operatorname{End} V — the algebra of endomorphisms of a vector space V over k (i.e., linear maps, or operators, from V to itself). The multiplication is given by composition of operators.4. The free algebra A = k\\langle x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\rangle. A basis of this algebra consists of words in letters x_1, \\ldots, x_n, and multiplication in this basis is simply the concatenation of words.5. The group algebra A = k[G] of a group G. Its basis is \\{a_g, g \\in G\\}, with multiplication law a_g a_h = a_{gh}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.2. Algebras\tExamples of algebras over k","header":"Examples of algebras over k","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Examples-of-algebras-over-k/#Chapter2___Example2___2___4"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/#chapter-04___section-4-6":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results","header":"4.6. Unitary representations. Another proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/#chapter-04___section-4-6"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/#chapter-09":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory","header":"9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/#chapter-09"}});
